{
  "id": "ep004",
  "duration": 49.676,
  "beats": [
    {
      "start": 0.15,
      "end": 4.467,
      "text": "The first pictures of the Moon's far side used American spy film.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Luna 3 moon.jpg",
        "credit": "OKB-1",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luna_3_moon.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 4.467,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 4.467,
      "end": 11.836,
      "text": "In October 1959, the Soviet Union launched Luna 3 to reveal our satellite's hidden hemisphere.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "N1 1M1 mockup on the launch pad at the Baikonur Cosmodrome in late 1967.jpg",
        "credit": "National Reconnaissance Office",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:N1_1M1_mockup_on_the_launch_pad_at_the_Baikonur_Cosmodrome_in_late_1967.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 4.467,
          "end": 11.833,
          "kind": "cover",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 11.836,
      "end": 17.556,
      "text": "Because the Moon is tidally locked, humanity had never seen the side facing away from Earth.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "A New View of the Moon.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_New_View_of_the_Moon.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 11.833,
          "end": 15.8,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 15.8,
          "end": 17.567,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 17.556,
      "end": 23.377,
      "text": "The probe carried an automated darkroom to develop, fix, and dry photographs mid-flight.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Luna 9 Space Probe 1.jpg",
        "credit": "Stolbovsky",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luna_9_Space_Probe_1.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 17.567,
          "end": 20.467,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 20.467,
          "end": 23.367,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 23.377,
      "end": 30.347,
      "text": "On October 7, 1959, the spacecraft took twenty-nine photographs over roughly forty minutes.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Moon Seen From the East (4989807909).jpg",
        "credit": "NASA Goddard Space Flight Center from Greenbelt, MD, USA",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moon_Seen_From_the_East_(4989807909).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 23.367,
          "end": 30.333,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 30.347,
      "end": 38.272,
      "text": "According to historical accounts, the radiation-resistant film inside the camera came from downed US spy balloons.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Moon Seen From the East (4989807909).jpg",
        "credit": "NASA Goddard Space Flight Center from Greenbelt, MD, USA",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moon_Seen_From_the_East_(4989807909).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 30.333,
          "end": 38.267,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 38.272,
      "end": 44.467,
      "text": "Seventeen frames survived transmission back to Earth, permanently altering our map of the Moon.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Moon Seen From the East (4989807909).jpg",
        "credit": "NASA Goddard Space Flight Center from Greenbelt, MD, USA",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moon_Seen_From_the_East_(4989807909).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 38.267,
          "end": 44.467,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 44.467,
      "end": 49.326,
      "text": "That is how American spy film captured the first pictures of the Moon's far side.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Luna 3 moon.jpg",
        "credit": "OKB-1",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luna_3_moon.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 44.467,
          "end": 49.667,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
